      <h1>How to Read a Pay Stub: Every Line Item and Tax Code Explained</h1>
      <div class="article-meta"><span>Sep 24, 2026</span><span>·</span><span>8 min read</span></div>
      <p class="article-intro">When you open your electronic pay stub from ADP, Gusto, Paychex, or Workday, it's packed with cryptic acronyms like OASDI, MED/EE, FITW, and YTD. Here is how to decode every single line.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="container" style="max-width:740px;">
    <div class="prose">
      <div class="callout"><strong>Why Reviewing Your Pay Stub Matters:</strong> Payroll errors occur more frequently than most workers realize. Miscalculated hourly rates, missed overtime, or incorrect benefit withholdings can quietly cost you hundreds of dollars every month if left uncorrected.</div>

      <h2>1. The Four Core Sections of a Pay Stub</h2>
      <p>Most corporate pay stubs are organized into four standard sections:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>General Information:</strong> Employer name, employee name, pay period dates (e.g., Sep 1 - Sep 14), and pay date.</li>
        <li><strong>Earnings / Gross Pay:</strong> Hours worked, pay rate, overtime, PTO hours, and total wages earned before deductions.</li>
        <li><strong>Deductions (Taxes & Benefits):</strong> All money subtracted from your earnings by law or by personal election.</li>
        <li><strong>Net Pay & Totals:</strong> The final amount deposited into your checking account, plus Year-to-Date (YTD) aggregates.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>2. Common Paystub Abbreviations Decoded</h2>
      <p>Here is what the most confusing abbreviations on your pay stub actually stand for:</p>
      <table>
        <tr><th>Abbreviation</th><th>Full Name</th><th>What It Means</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>FED W/H / FIT</strong></td><td>Federal Income Tax Withholding</td><td>Taxes sent to the IRS based on your W-4 brackets</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>OASDI / SOC SEC</strong></td><td>Old-Age, Survivors, and Disability Insurance</td><td>The official legal name for Social Security (6.2%)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>MED / FICA MED</strong></td><td>Medicare Tax</td><td>Federal health insurance fund tax (1.45%)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>SIT / ST W/H</strong></td><td>State Income Tax</td><td>Withholding sent to your state department of revenue</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>SDI / SUI</strong></td><td>State Disability / Unemployment</td><td>Mandatory in CA, NY, NJ, RI, and HI for wage protection</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>YTD</strong></td><td>Year-to-Date</td><td>Total money earned or deducted since Jan 1 of this year</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>401(k) / PRE-TAX</strong></td><td>Retirement Contribution</td><td>Pre-tax savings that lower your taxable gross income</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>ROTH 401(k)</strong></td><td>Post-Tax Retirement</td><td>Retirement savings deducted AFTER taxes have been calculated</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>HSA / FSA</strong></td><td>Health / Flexible Spending</td><td>Pre-tax accounts for medical expenses or childcare</td></tr>
      </table>

      <h2>3. Pre-Tax vs. Post-Tax Deductions: What's the Difference?</h2>
      <p>Pay stubs separate deductions into two critical categories:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Pre-Tax Deductions:</strong> Taken out of your check <em>before</em> income taxes are calculated. This lowers your taxable income, saving you money in taxes. Examples include traditional 401(k), health/dental premiums, and commuter benefits.</li>
        <li><strong>Post-Tax Deductions:</strong> Taken out <em>after</em> taxes are calculated. Examples include Roth 401(k) contributions, wage garnishments, union dues, and supplemental life insurance.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>4. Red Flags to Check on Every Pay Stub</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Pay Rate Accuracy:</strong> Confirm your hourly rate or base salary matches your latest review or promotion letter.</li>
        <li><strong>Overtime Multipliers:</strong> Hours over 40 must be compensated at 1.5× your regular rate under FLSA regulations.</li>
        <li><strong>Marital & Filing Status:</strong> Ensure your W-4 withholding status (Single vs Married) matches your current tax filing plan.</li>
      </ul>

